"""Curated project handbook. Technical plan, not robot control software."""
PAGES={}
def page(key,title,lead,body): PAGES[key]={'title':title,'lead':lead,'body':body}
page('index','A small farm, built one working loop at a time.','The current plan in one place: build the farm software on the Mac now, then connect and commission the robot in Japan from September 30.', '''
<div class="actions"><a class="button" href="software.html">Read the software roadmap</a><a class="button secondary" href="assembly.html">Assemble the robot step by step</a><a class="button secondary" href="planters.html">Understand the printed kits</a></div>
<div class="grid three"><article class="card"><span class="badge">Done · Sep 27</span><h3>The farm program exists</h3><p>Adapters, LLM-taught skills, perception, care cycle, evidence store, viewer, Jev/Astra authority ladder. 23 simulator tests pass; real-model checks done. <a href="https://github.com/RonTuretzky/xlerobot-farm/tree/main/software">software/</a></p></article><article class="card"><span class="badge">Next · Japan</span><h3>Commission one care cycle</h3><p>Park the robot. Inspect, measure, make a supervised bottle pour, verify the outcome and recover from interruptions.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="badge">Later · gated</span><h3>Earn more autonomy</h3><p>Task demonstrations, held-out evaluation, longer observation windows and only then additional care or mobility.</p></article></div>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Status, September 27:</strong> no planter parts have been printed and no growing test has happened (the NY K1 Max jammed). The printer now available is an <strong>AnkerMake M5C</strong> (220 × 220 × 250 mm): the cress planter and the new nest/paddle/bottle-rest parts fit; the square kit's 220 mm outer tray does not. The farm software is written and tested on its simulator; hardware validation starts when the robot is assembled on September 30. Nobody drives the robot: motion is taught by the vision model, decisions go rules → Jev → a person only when needed.</div>
<h2>What we have decided</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Current direction</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Watering</td><td>A small robot-held pouring bottle. Human refill.</td><td>Chosen; grip/spout and accurate pours untested.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Growing</td><td>Rectangular wicking planter and square covered germination kit. Each can finish a crop.</td><td>Not printed yet; printer jammed. No leak/wick test has been done.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sensing</td><td>One removable light paddle. Moisture probe and relay deferred for paper trays.</td><td>Recommended first build; human water/wetness checks before refills.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cameras</td><td>Use the robot’s head/wrist cameras initially.</td><td>No separate overhead camera in the first build.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Computer</td><td>The Mac connects to robot interfaces, cameras and ESP32 through the owned powered USB hub.</td><td>Physical connections still to commission.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Battery</td><td>Anker C300 DC received. Not used in V0; robot runs on its wall supplies while parked.</td><td>12 V harness from the car outlet still undesigned and unfused.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Furniture</td><td>Free fully printed modular table for the planters; separate cart for the robot.</td><td>Table chosen; editable model files still missing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Software</td><td>Eight-layer farm program on the Mac over LeRobot 0.6: adapters, LLM-servo skills, perception, care cycle, evidence, viewer, Jev/Astra authority ladder.</td><td>Written Sep 27; 23 simulator tests pass; Claude CLI, Jev and Astra verified live. Untested on the physical robot until Sep 30. <a href="software.html">Deck</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operation</td><td>No human operation of the robot. Keyframes are taught by the vision model (LLM-servo); a person only answers questions in the viewer.</td><td>Decided Sep 27. Servo loop verified against the simulator with the real model.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trays</td><td>Fixed nests on the table: printed locating plate per cress planter with an AprilTag recess; printed light paddle and bottle rest.</td><td>Parts generated for the M5C; unprinted.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<figure><img src="assets/assembly-v3.png" alt="Robot cart beside a modular table carrying the two planter kits"><figcaption>Actual planter meshes; conceptual table/robot placement. Bottle spout is a placeholder. Height, reach and structural capacity are untested.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>The next three useful actions</h2><ol><li><a href="prints.html#queue">Print on the M5C</a>: the cress planter (6 pieces), two nest plates, the light paddle and the bottle rest. About one day of printing.</li><li>Flash the ESP32 firmware and run <code>farm sim --auto-answer</code> on the Mac to see the whole program and the viewer before the robot arrives.</li><li>September 30: assemble, <code>farm devices</code> → <code>farm calibrate</code> → <code>farm check</code> → <code>farm teach-all</code> → empty-bottle <code>farm once</code>. <a href="software.html#timeline">Build order</a>.</li></ol>
<div class="callout"><strong>Removed from V0:</strong> electric watering pump, external reservoir, hoses/float switches, scale hardware, separate overhead camera and leak pads. The requested silicone mat, adhesive rubber sheets, plant labels and measuring syringes remain out. Use household measuring materials.</div>
<p class="small">This handbook resolves newer decisions over the original deck/redline. “Selected”, “printed”, “connected” and “validated” mean different things; every unresolved fit is listed where it matters. Last consolidated September 26, 2026.</p>
''')
page('ny-trial','The first growing trial has not started.','Nothing was printed in New York and no seeds were sown. These instructions apply to the first crop wherever it happens: in Japan with printed kits, or in a household tray if the prints are still missing.', '''
<div class="toc"><a href="#timing">Status</a><a href="#start">Setup</a><a href="#daily">Daily care</a><a href="#trouble">Troubleshooting</a><a href="#journal">Trial notebook</a></div>
<section id="timing"><h2>Where the trial stands</h2><div class="callout warn"><strong>September 26:</strong> the planned New York pre-trial did not happen. The K1 Max jammed with PLA in the nozzle after the first job was aborted, so no planter part is available and no leak, wick or germination test has been run. The cress seed packet was added to the cart but its purchase is unconfirmed. Everything below is a plan for the first trial, not a record of one.</div><p>Garden cress (<em>Lepidium sativum</em>) suits a small paper-based trial. A seed supplier gives roughly <strong>2–4 days to germinate and 7–10 days to harvest</strong>; real timing varies. <a href="https://seedmart.com.au/how-to-grow-cress-microgreens-step-by-step-guide-for-beginners/">Seedmart’s cress guide</a>. This is a planning estimate, not a guarantee for any particular packet.</p><p><strong>If the kits are not printed by sowing day:</strong> use a shallow household dish with household paper. The robot software does not depend on the printed geometry for its first supervised pours; it depends on a tray in a repeatable position with a visible refill opening. Seeds must be bought locally in Japan; carrying seed or growing material through customs is not planned.</p></section>
<section id="start"><h2>Your first setup, step by step</h2><p><strong>No moisture electronics are required:</strong> check water reserve and paper wetness by hand for this trial. <a href="hardware.html#moisture-decision">Why we recommend deferring the soil probe</a>.</p><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Use what you already have</h3><ul><li>The printed cress planter or square kit once they exist; otherwise a shallow household dish.</li><li>Fresh garden-cress seed. Use untreated seed intended for growing; check the delivered packet.</li><li>Plain, unscented household kitchen paper and clean water.</li><li>A clean kitchen tray/dish underneath, a small cup or spoon, and a phone camera.</li><li>A household loose cover if the cress planter needs humidity retention; square kit includes its cover.</li></ul><p>No growing soil, fertilizer, purchased mats, sensors or robot needed for this short paper trial.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>What “self-watering” means</h3><p>The rectangular planter stores a little water below the seeds. Paper carries it upward by capillary action. You still check it and refill by hand.</p><p>The square kit has a cover and removable insert, but it does not automatically wick unless its growing medium makes the needed connection to water.</p><p><a href="planters.html">See the planter renders</a></p></article></div>
<div class="steps"><div class="step"><h3>Inspect, rinse and leak-test</h3><p>Remove loose plastic, rinse with cool water and check the fit without forcing clips. Put the empty trough/outer tray on dry kitchen paper inside an existing dish. Add a modest amount of water below the growing surface. Inspect after 30 minutes and again later that day. Any wet patch underneath fails the test: use another dish for growing while repairing/reprinting.</p><p class="small">The 30-minute check is our proposed initial screen, not a leak certification. PLA prints may retain residue and are not established food-contact equipment. Treat unknown-filament prints as growth/mechanical tests; use a suitable food-growing container for any batch intended to eat. Avoid boiling water or a hot dishwasher on the PLA parts.</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Make the wick actually touch the water</h3><p>For the rectangular planter: snap its four insets into the holder. Feed paper down into each inset and lay 3–4 layers across the seed platform, maintaining a continuous wet-paper path. Pre-wet the paper, drain surface puddles and fill the trough below the seed platform. The creator suggests about 80% full; don’t use that as an excuse to submerge seeds.</p><p>For the square kit: put damp paper on the perforated insert inside its outer tray. Keep it moist from above initially; only rely on bottom water after confirming paper/root contact or a working wick. Water underneath an air gap cannot wet the paper automatically.</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Sow a small, even patch</h3><p>Scatter dry cress seeds onto moist paper, with no thick piles. Do not pre-soak cress: it becomes sticky when wet. One packet is enough for small test patches; there is no need to densely fill the entire large tray. Give both kits similar seeded patch areas and density, using the same seed lot. Keep an unseeded edge on the same sheet for moisture checks.</p><p>Use a phone note or tape already at home to label A = wick planter, B = square tray, and optionally C = household dish. A household reference can help distinguish seed trouble from a printed-container problem; it is not a controlled scientific experiment.</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Keep the first stage damp, then give it light</h3><p>Follow the seed packet’s conditions. A loose cover can reduce early drying; check at least morning and evening and do not seal it airtight. Remove the cover when shoots emerge and begin standing up; use the plant’s condition rather than an automatic date.</p><p>Then place it in bright natural light away from intense window heat, radiators and cold drafts. An ordinary comfortable room is a practical starting point. Leave airflow around the seedlings. Never leave a covered tray baking in direct sun.</p></div></div>
<p class="small">Assembly steps adapted from <a href="https://www.printables.com/model/434505-self-watering-cress-or-microgreens-planter">Macce’s planter instructions</a>; cress sowing/early growth from <a href="https://seedmart.com.au/how-to-grow-cress-microgreens-step-by-step-guide-for-beginners/">Seedmart</a>. Our leak screens, comparisons and log schedule are proposed tests. The tray setup is an adaptation to the actual printed kit, not a claim that the seed supplier tested these prints.</p></section>
<section id="daily"><figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/growth-stages.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/growth-stages.png" alt="Three Blender stages from seeded paper to green seedlings"></a><figcaption>Illustrative observations, not a promised timetable. Log what your crop actually does. Open the image for a larger view.</figcaption></figure><h2>The first six days of any trial</h2><div class="day"><h3>Day 0 · fit, water and sow</h3><p>Complete the leak/wick checks, photograph the dry assembly and first sowing. Record crop, date, paper layers, seeded area and starting water level. Measure water with kitchen utensils only if practical; otherwise record “not measured”.</p></div><div class="day"><h3>Days 1–2 · check the water path</h3><p>Morning and evening: check the trough, paper at several spots, and the dry surface underneath. Add only enough water to keep paper evenly damp. Photograph from the same angle. Some seed may swell or begin rooting; slower emergence is still possible.</p></div><div class="day"><h3>Days 2–4 · look for emergence</h3><p>When shoots establish, uncover and give bright light. Log first roots, upright shoots and seed leaves. If a patch is dry while the trough has water, fix wick contact instead of adding more water blindly.</p></div><div class="day"><h3>Days 4–5 · test useful repeatability</h3><p>Compare wetness across the tray, count manual top-ups and note any leak or wilt. Practice tiny hand pours at the refill opening using your kitchen measure. Do not deliberately drought-stress the live crop; test dry/damp/wet sensor samples separately if electronics are available.</p></div><div class="day"><h3>Day 6 · record and review</h3><p>Take final photos, compare the two kits, list changes and export the notebook. Decide whether to continue the crop or end it and clean/dry the prints.</p></div>
<div class="callout"><strong>What a first trial can prove:</strong> visible germination, a reliable water path, no observed leaks, a known manual refill routine and photos/notes explaining failures. This does not establish autonomous care, edible-product safety or a guaranteed number of unattended days.</div></section>
<section id="trouble"><h2>What a problem looks like</h2><details><summary>There is water below, but the paper is dry</summary><p>The paper may not reach the reservoir, the wick may have separated, or the tray may be tilted. Re-wet and reconnect it. Check multiple points. Don’t drown the seeds to compensate for an air gap.</p></details><details><summary>Seeds are floating or the surface is puddled</summary><p>Remove excess water gently and lower the water level. Keep the paper damp, not submerged. Avoid blasting seeds with a direct pour.</p></details><details><summary>The shoots are pale or leaning</summary><p>Uncover after emergence and improve light without overheating. Use the same camera angle to track changes; brightness in a phone photo alone is not a light-meter reading.</p></details><details><summary>Nothing has emerged by day 4</summary><p>Check seed identity/packet guidance, freshness, paper moisture and temperature. Compare the household reference if you started one. Don’t keep increasing water; saturated seeds can also fail.</p></details><details><summary>Slime, fuzzy growth or an unpleasant smell</summary><p>Cress produces clear gel around wet seeds. Root hairs can look fuzzy, while spreading fuzzy patches, discoloration or a bad smell warrant stopping and discarding the affected trial. A photo alone may not distinguish them reliably. Don’t eat an uncertain batch; clean the container before restarting.</p></details></section>
<section id="journal"><h2>Your trial notebook</h2><p>Checks and observations stay in this browser. They are not uploaded, synced, or sent to the robot. Export before changing devices or clearing browser storage.</p><label for="trial-start">Sowing date</label><input id="trial-start" type="date"><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="leak">Leak check passed at the tested water level</label><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="wick">Paper stayed damp through an observed interval</label><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="sprout">First sprouts photographed</label><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="light">Cover removed after emergence; seedlings in light</label><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="handoff">End-of-trial care or cleanup arranged</label><form id="journal-form"><div class="grid"><div><label for="kit">Kit</label><select id="kit" name="kit"><option>Wicking planter A</option><option>Square tray B</option><option>Household reference C</option></select></div><div><label for="stage">Growth stage</label><select id="stage" name="stage"><option>Pre-sowing test</option><option>Seeds sown</option><option>Roots emerging</option><option>Shoots upright</option><option>Green seed leaves</option><option>Problem / trial ended</option></select></div><div><label for="moisture">Paper condition</label><select id="moisture" name="moisture"><option>Evenly damp</option><option>Dry patches</option><option>Puddled / too wet</option><option>Not checked</option></select></div><div><label for="water">Water added (mL, leave blank if unmeasured)</label><input id="water" name="water" type="number" min="0" step="0.1"></div></div><label for="notes">Observation / photo filename</label><textarea id="notes" name="notes" rows="3" maxlength="3000" placeholder="What changed? Where is the photo saved?"></textarea><div class="actions"><button type="submit">Save observation</button><button type="button" id="export-notes">Export notebook JSON</button></div></form><p id="save-status" class="small" role="status">Stored locally in this browser only.</p><div id="journal-entries"></div></section>
''')
page('planters','Two kits. Two water paths.','They are alternatives, not a compulsory germinate-then-transplant sequence. Keep the crop in whichever kit you sowed it in.', '''
<div class="toc"><a href="#wick">Rectangular planter</a><a href="#square">Square kit</a><a href="#robot">Robot access</a></div>
<section id="wick"><h2>Rectangular self-watering cress planter</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><span class="badge">6 printed pieces</span><h3>What each piece does</h3><ul><li><strong>Trough:</strong> stores the small supply of water.</li><li><strong>Holder:</strong> supports the growing paper above it.</li><li><strong>Four insets:</strong> hold paper down into water and make wick paths. They are not four separate crop pots.</li></ul><p>Trough envelope from the downloaded STL: approximately 180 × 85 × 25 mm. Paper and water are not printed parts.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Capillary action does the work</h3><p>A continuous sheet of paper carries water from the trough to the seed platform. Four insets keep the paper in contact with water.</p><p>Refill before the wick loses contact. Passive watering reduces intervention; it does not guarantee the planter can be left alone for six days.</p></article></div><figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/wick-cutaway.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/wick-cutaway.png" alt="Blender cutaway of the actual printed wicking planter"></a><figcaption>Actual creator meshes cut open for explanation. Household paper connects the seed platform to the stored water; the four insets are wick holders. The cutaway is not a print file. Open the image for a larger view.</figcaption></figure><p><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/434505-self-watering-cress-or-microgreens-planter">Original by Macce · CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. The creator uses household paper and printed PETG; our PLA version needs its own leak and durability checks. <a href="ny-trial.html#start">Follow the start guide</a>.</p></section>
<section id="square"><h2>Square germination tray kit</h2><figure><img src="assets/germination-exploded-v3.png" alt="Three separated parts: cover A, perforated growing insert B and outer water tray C"><figcaption>Original downloaded meshes, separated in Blender to explain their roles. Cover orientation depends on its intended use; use it over the growing insert for early germination.</figcaption></figure><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Function</th><th>Downloaded envelope</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A · Cover / part 3</td><td>Retains humidity and blocks light initially; remove for the green growth stage. Weighted blackout is crop-specific and not needed for our first cress trial.</td><td>205 × 205 × 35 mm</td></tr><tr><td>B · Insert / part 2</td><td>Holds medium/seeds; perforations provide drainage/root access.</td><td>195 × 195 × 35 mm</td></tr><tr><td>C · Outer tray / part 1</td><td>Supports the insert and catches or holds water.</td><td>220 × 220 × 50 mm</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These measured STL bounds differ from the listing’s nominal dimensions. Use the downloaded dimensions for print planning. Seed on a suitable moist medium in B; C is not a signal to flood the seeds. If there is an air gap to bottom water, top-wet initially and verify the medium’s water path.</p><p><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/1287698-microgreens-germination-tray-kit">Original by HomestteadAce · CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. Both kits can stay on an ordinary stable household surface for the New York trial.</p></section>
<section id="robot"><h2>How the robot joins this later</h2><ol><li>Recognize the tray and the saved watering target while its base is parked.</li><li>Inspect the uncovered crop; a person checks water reserve and wetness at an unseeded paper edge. The contact probe is optional.</li><li>Use one removable light paddle at plant height as a separate operation.</li><li>Take a small bottle, pour a tested amount at an accessible target and return upright.</li><li>Observe wetness/spills and save evidence. Uncertain delivery pauses the cycle.</li></ol><p>Manual lid removal, bottle refill and tool loading are acceptable V0 steps. The cress refill hole is narrow; the square tray’s access around its fitted insert also needs physical testing. Neither a render nor the upstream demo proves these targets are reachable with our bottle.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Why a water pocket if the robot waters?</strong> The pocket is inside the planter; the robot can refill it. We removed the separate powered irrigation system, not the plant’s ability to hold water.</div></section>
''')
page('prints','Nothing is printed yet. The printer is now an AnkerMake M5C.','AnkerMake M5C · 220 × 220 × 250 mm · 0.4 mm nozzle · PLA. The NY K1 Max jammed and produced nothing; the M5C is the machine the parts are now sized for.', '''
<div class="toc"><a href="#queue">Printer and queue</a><a href="#fit">What fits the M5C</a><a href="#nests">Nests, paddle, bottle rest</a><a href="#table">Modular table</a><a href="#fits">Unfinished attachments</a></div>
<section id="fit"><h2>What fits the AnkerMake M5C</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Envelope</th><th>M5C 220 × 220 × 250</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Cress planter trough (Macce)</td><td>≈ 180 × 85 × 25 mm</td><td>Fits.</td></tr><tr><td>Cress holder + four insets</td><td>smaller</td><td>Fit; all six pieces on one or two plates.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Square kit outer tray (HomestteadAce)</td><td>220 × 220 × 50 mm</td><td><strong>Does not fit</strong> (no margin for a skirt). Print at 95% scale with the insert and cover scaled the same, or drop the square kit.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Square kit insert / cover</td><td>195 × 195 · 205 × 205</td><td>Fit, but only useful with the outer tray.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nest plate, light paddle, bottle rest, tag tiles</td><td>≤ 200 × 105 mm</td><td>Designed for this bed. <a href="#nests">Details</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Modular table parts (Aj_Awesome1)</td><td>designed for a Prusa MINI, 180 × 180</td><td>Fit once the editable files are obtained.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The M5C accepts G-code from AnkerMake Studio, PrusaSlicer or Cura; use a generic 0.4 mm PLA profile, 0.2 mm layers, 3 walls, 20% infill, 210–220 °C. Sources: <a href="https://www.fabbaloo.com/news/hands-on-with-the-ankermake-m5c-3d-printer-part-1">Fabbaloo hands-on</a>, <a href="https://3dprintingindustry.com/news/ankermake-m5c-review-easy-affordable-and-reliable-3d-printing-226560/">3D Printing Industry review</a>.</p></section>
<section id="queue"><h2>Printer status</h2><div class="callout warn"><strong>No planter part exists.</strong> The September 22 K1 Max jobs did not produce usable parts; that printer now clicks while feeding (nozzle clog). The earlier claims on this page that a tray had completed are withdrawn; nothing has been fit-, leak- or wick-tested. Printing continues on the M5C.</div><h3>K1 Max unjam procedure (if it is used again)</h3><ol><li>Clear the bed. Heat the nozzle to about 220–230 °C from the touchscreen.</li><li>Push a <strong>0.4 mm cleaning needle</strong> up into the nozzle tip from below and work it a few times.</li><li>Extrude a short length by hand. A smooth steady line means the clog is cleared.</li><li>If it still clicks, unload the filament and push the jam down from above with the long clog-poke rod while hot; failing that, swap the nozzle with the kit wrench while hot.</li></ol><p>Suitable kits: <a href="https://us.amazon.com/dp/B0D69MKJ2K">K1 nozzle kit with 0.4 mm needles, spare nozzles and wrench</a> or <a href="https://www.amazon.com/Cleaning-Kit-Cleaning-L-Shaped-Tool-Creality/dp/B0DDKYM92P">needle kit with clog-poke rod</a>. Check the nozzle style against your unit before relying on the spares. <a href="https://wiki.creality.com/en/k1-flagship-series/k1-max/troubleshooting/filament-feed-issue">Creality’s K1 Max clog procedure</a>.</p>
<h3>Queue once it prints again</h3><figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/both-kits-first.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/both-kits-first.png" alt="Seven parts arranged on one K1 Max bed: square insert and complete cress planter"></a><figcaption>Planned first plate: square insert plus the complete cress planter (sliced 10h06m, 274.84 g). Layout only; not printed.</figcaption></figure><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Plate</th><th>Contents</th><th>PLA / estimate</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>Square insert + cress trough, holder and four insets</td><td>274.84 g · 10h06m</td><td>Not printed. Start here.</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Germination outer tray</td><td>309.96 g · 10h10m</td><td>Not printed (the Sep 22 attempt is not counted as a finished part).</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>Germination cover</td><td>≈ 6h07m</td><td>Deferred; a loose household cover works first.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="downloads/farm-both-kits-first.3mf">Download plate A Creality project</a><a class="button secondary" href="downloads/farm-two-kits-three-plates.3mf" download>Original three-plate project</a><a class="button secondary" href="downloads/planter-source-stls.zip" download>Original planter STLs + credits</a></div><p><strong>Saved settings:</strong> 0.20 mm layers, three walls, five top/bottom layers, 15% infill, no supports; Hyper PLA preset, 220 °C nozzle, 50 °C bed. These are slicer settings, not proof of watertightness. Import with the K1 Max profile and inspect before slicing; no auto-run G-code is published here.</p><p>The growing trial no longer waits on these prints: a household dish can stand in for the first supervised pours if the kits are still missing on September 30.</p></section>
<section id="nests"><h2>Nests, tag tiles, light paddle, bottle rest</h2><p>The robot needs each tray in a known place. A printed <strong>nest plate</strong> (200 × 165 × 10 mm) locates the cress trough: a 181.5 × 86.5 mm pocket with three stepped ledges as a lead-in, a 6 mm lip, a 48 mm front apron carrying a 40.5 × 40.5 mm AprilTag recess and two M4 holes, two rear tabs, and a 30 mm notch on the short side so the bottle spout reaches the refill opening. <strong>Tag tiles</strong> are real AprilTag 36h11 id 1 and id 2 (40 × 40 × 1.6 mm, raised cells; make the raised cells black with a filament change at Z = 1.0 mm or a marker). The <strong>light paddle</strong> (150 × 24 × 8 mm) is a gripper handle with grooves, a BH1750 pocket and a cable channel; the <strong>bottle rest</strong> is Ø70 × 12 mm with a 56 mm bore and a 40° lead-in. All fit the M5C bed and print flat without supports.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>STL</th><th>Size (mm)</th><th>Print</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="downloads/parts/nest_cress.stl" download>nest_cress.stl</a></td><td>200 × 165 × 10</td><td>× 2 (trays A and B)</td></tr><tr><td><a href="downloads/parts/tag_36h11_id1.stl" download>tag_36h11_id1.stl</a> · <a href="downloads/parts/tag_36h11_id2.stl" download>id2</a></td><td>40 × 40 × 1.6</td><td>× 1 each; black the raised cells</td></tr><tr><td><a href="downloads/parts/paddle_bh1750.stl" download>paddle_bh1750.stl</a></td><td>150 × 24 × 8</td><td>× 1</td></tr><tr><td><a href="downloads/parts/bottle_rest.stl" download>bottle_rest.stl</a></td><td>70 × 70 × 12</td><td>× 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>0.2 mm layers, 3 walls, 20% infill, PLA 210–220 °C. Generated by <a href="https://github.com/RonTuretzky/xlerobot-farm/tree/main/software/parts"><code>software/parts/make_parts.py</code></a>; <a href="downloads/parts/README.md">its README</a> lists ten assumptions to verify against the real bottle (54 mm assumed), BH1750 hole spacing (14.5 mm assumed) and trough before trusting the fits. The square kit gets no nest: one would exceed the bed.</p></section>
<section id="table"><h2>Chosen: Modular Table (100% printed)</h2><p><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/177153-modular-table-100-printed/files">Aj_Awesome1’s free CC0 design</a> uses printed top tiles, rods and snap connectors. This is the selected direction for a full expandable table with legs. The robot remains on its separate cart; the table only carries the growing kits.</p><div class="callout warn"><strong>File blocker:</strong> the received <code>print_files.zip</code> contains five pre-sliced Prusa MINI G-code files and a PDF. We still need <strong>ALL MODEL FILES (538 KB)</strong>, the 13 editable 3MF models. Do not run the MINI files on the K1 Max.</div><p>The Blender table is a labeled 640 × 400 × 650 mm layout proxy, not the actual designer’s joints. Final dimensions, connector orientation, wet load, stability, long-term PLA sag and robot reach are untested. Import original parts before creating a real print quantity list. No reliable table filament/time estimate exists yet.</p><figure><img src="assets/assembly-v3.png" alt="Conceptual modular table beside robot cart with actual planter models"><figcaption>Planter geometry is real; table and robot envelopes illustrate placement. A table render is not manufacturing approval.</figcaption></figure><a class="button secondary" href="downloads/farm-modular-table-v3.blend" download>Download current Blender assembly</a></section>
<section id="fits"><h2>Small printed parts still to fit</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Moisture probe holder</h3><p>Optional experiment, deferred for the paper-tray build. Carrier and clamp prototypes exist. If revisited, add a depth stop referenced to the tray rim and gentle compliance before touching thin paper. Measure the delivered board, gripper and growing depth; don’t assume the rigid prototype is ready.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Light paddle</h3><p>One grippable carrier holds one BH1750 face upward. Keep the sensing face clear of the arm/cable shadow; strain-relieve the moving cable. Tool is parked when watering.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Bottle rest and spout</h3><p>The parked bottle needs repeatable position. The Nalgene wide-mouth container needs a controlled outlet or a replacement household bottle. Shown spout is a placeholder, not an exported working part.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Dry enclosure and locating stops</h3><p>Keep ESP32/boards dry, secure leads and locate the planters repeatably. Cable routes, tool pockets and fasteners depend on measured hardware.</p></article></div><p><a href="downloads/tool-prototypes-v2.zip" download>Download V2 tool prototypes</a> for fit evaluation. The old short pod decks/legs are superseded by the selected full-table direction; they are retained only in the historical design, not part of this print recommendation.</p></section>
''')
page('hardware','One Mac. Separate motor and sensor connections.','The first build keeps the robot parked and uses its existing cameras. Sensors attach through an ESP32; USB does not power the robot’s motors.', '''
<div class="actions"><a class="button" href="assembly.html">Open the 16-step robot assembly guide</a></div>
<section id="moisture-decision"><h2>Do these microgreen trays need the soil-moisture probe?</h2><div class="callout"><strong>No, not for the initial paper-grown trial. We recommend deferring the probe and its relay.</strong> Keep one light paddle if you want robotic light measurements. The planters themselves need no electronics.</div><figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/wick-cutaway.png"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/wick-cutaway.png" alt="Paper carries water from the cress trough to the growing surface"></a><figcaption>The cress trough stores water below the growing surface. Paper must remain connected to that water. Water in the trough and wetness at the seeds are two separate checks.</figcaption></figure><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>First-build check</th><th>What the probe would tell us</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Is there water left below the plants?</td><td>A person checks the accessible refill area; record a photo when visible.</td><td>Touching the paper does not measure water depth.</td></tr><tr><td>Is water reaching the growing surface?</td><td>Inspect a seed-free edge and check paper wetness by hand with clean hands, without disturbing roots. Repeat at more than one area.</td><td>Conductivity only at the contact patch, if contact is repeatable.</td></tr><tr><td>Should the robot refill?</td><td>A person reviews both checks and authorizes a small tested pour.</td><td>A reading alone cannot establish the needed dose or rule out wick failure.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The <a href="https://learn.sparkfun.com/tutorials/soil-moisture-sensor-hookup-guide">SparkFun guide</a> describes a resistive conductivity measurement and recommends powering only for readings to reduce corrosion. On our thin paper, contact pressure, contact area and water chemistry can change the result. That makes it an optional experiment, not a validated requirement. It might become useful with thicker growing media or a demonstrably repeatable paper contact method.</p><p><strong>For the square kit:</strong> do not assume the perforated insert wicks by itself. Establish the paper/mat’s capillary connection by hand before depending on water beneath it. A full lower tray can coexist with a dry growing surface.</p><p><strong>For future unattended care:</strong> removing the probe does not make cameras a proven replacement. Water-level visibility, a validated level sensor/indicator, or another independent measurement could be evaluated later. A hidden waterline or uncertain wetness pauses automatic watering. Neither a level reading alone nor a timer proves that the wick is working.</p><p><strong>Shopping implication:</strong> propose deferring the $14.45 probe and $8.99 relay pack ($23.44 at the last recorded prices). The Amazon cart has not been changed in this update. ESP32, one BH1750 and its four-wire harness still serve light measurements; if you omit all external sensors, that entire extra circuit can also be deferred.</p></section>
<h2>The connected workbench</h2><figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/workbench.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/workbench.png" alt="Blender workbench with Mac, powered hub, cameras, motor interfaces and ESP32"></a><figcaption>The Mac uses the powered hub for USB connections to the robot motor interfaces, existing cameras and ESP32. Robot adapters supply motor power separately; the hub has its own adapter. Equipment shapes and cable routes are illustrative. Open the image for a larger view.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The project selected the <a href="https://shop.wowrobo.com/products/xlerobot-dual-arm-mobile-household-robot-kit">WowRobo XLeRobot Combo</a>. Recorded kit contents include motor interfaces, three cameras, robot cables and two 12V adapters; verify the delivered boxes and labels. Hub and Anker C300 DC were confirmed bought. The laptop is the current computer plan; no extra Pi is required just to run the sensor logger. Actual macOS drivers, camera bandwidth and robot-software compatibility still need a bench check.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Latest cart gap to resolve by reuse:</strong> the separate USB-A to USB-C data cable is no longer in the active cart. Have a suitable existing data cable ready for the ESP32; a charge-only cable will not carry readings. The cart’s removal does not prove it was purchased. Likewise, arrange existing cable ties/fasteners/insulation as needed.</div>
<h2>Each electrical part, in plain language</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>ESP32: the sensor translator</h3><figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/usb-link.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/usb-link.png" alt="Blender close-up of a USB data cable between hub and ESP32"></a><figcaption>A USB data cable connects the powered hub to the ESP32. Sensor records return over USB serial through the hub to the Mac. Open the image for a larger view.</figcaption></figure><p>One board reads sensors; the three-pack gives spares. The robot’s motor boards do not replace it. Develop and test the serial protocol on the bench before attaching anything to a moving arm.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Light: four connections</h3><figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/light-tool.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/light-tool.png" alt="Actual printed light paddle with a representative BH1750 and flexible harness"></a><figcaption>Four connections: verified 3.3V-compatible supply, common ground, SDA data and SCL clock. Actual holder mesh; board geometry and cable routing are illustrative. Open the image for a larger view.</figcaption></figure><p>Confirm the breakout’s pin labels/supply requirements. Use a 3.3V-compatible bus. Route a flexible four-wire cable to one removable paddle. Test errors through the complete arm motion; long I²C wiring is not automatically reliable.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Optional experiment: local wetness</h3><figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/probe-contact.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/probe-contact.png" alt="Blender view of the moisture probe touching a seed-free paper patch"></a><figcaption>Three connections: switched 3.3V to probe VCC, common ground to GND, and SIG to an ESP32 ADC input. Contact pose is illustrative; depth stop and compliant mounting still need tests. Open the image for a larger view.</figcaption></figure><p>The SparkFun SEN-13637 measures conductivity, affected by contact and dissolved salts. It is a local wetness proxy, not a reservoir level meter. Keep signal within the ESP32 input range; never feed 5V into its ADC.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Deferred with probe: power relay</h3><figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/relay-physical.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/relay-physical.png" alt="Blender layout of ESP32, relay module and probe power terminal"></a><figcaption>ESP32 output controls module IN; a verified 5V supply powers the module, with common ground. Confirm the delivered module trigger and supply configuration. Open the image for a larger view.</figcaption></figure><figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/relay-cutaway.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/relay-cutaway.png" alt="Blender cutaway illustrating a normally open relay contact"></a><figcaption>Contact side: 3.3V supply at COM, probe VCC at normally open (NO). Separate coil power operates the switch. This is a conceptual mechanism, not an exact model of the purchased relay. Open the image for a larger view.</figcaption></figure><p>Only if the probe experiment is reinstated: use one relay channel; no pump is switched. Verify the delivered module’s trigger configuration, supply current and boot-off behavior. Contact side switches 3.3V; relay coil supply is separate. Final GPIOs and power pin selection wait for board pinout verification.</p></article></div>
<p class="small">These renders explain placement and purpose; the captions preserve functional connections. They are not an approved pin-by-pin harness. Read the board labels and <a href="https://learn.sparkfun.com/tutorials/soil-moisture-sensor-hookup-guide/all">SparkFun hookup guide</a>; check voltage/continuity before plugging the sensor harness into the computer.</p>
<h2>The dry assembly bench</h2><figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/electronics-parts.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/electronics-parts.png" alt="Blender layout of boards, breadboard, perfboard and flexible wire"></a><figcaption>Start on a dry breadboard, then transfer verified connections to retained connectors/perfboard. One light module serves the arm. Moisture equipment shown is optional and deferred; optional air sensing stays off the arm. Open the image for a larger view.</figcaption></figure><h2>What needs soldering?</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Soldering task</th><th>What connects afterward</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESP32 / BH1750 / optional SHT31</td><td>If headers arrive loose, solder the header pins into the board’s plated holes. Already fitted headers need none.</td><td>Jumper plugs for bench tests; retained connectors for final build.</td></tr><tr><td>Perfboard + terminal kit</td><td>Solder headers and screw-terminal pins to perfboard, plus planned interconnects.</td><td>Strip wire ends and clamp under terminal screws.</td></tr><tr><td>Screw-terminal moisture probe</td><td>No solder at the probe terminal if the block arrives fitted.</td><td>Three wire ends into VCC/GND/SIG.</td></tr><tr><td>Moving wire splices</td><td>Only if extending/joining wires that way.</td><td>Insulate exposed joints with heat-shrink or use suitable enclosed connectors.</td></tr><tr><td>USB cables, robot kit leads, bottle, planters</td><td>No added soldering for their ordinary connections.</td><td>Plug-in or mechanical assembly.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>Optional probe experiment: calibration and contact testing</h2><p><strong>Calibration</strong> means recording what your installed probe reads on the actual paper with known dry, damp and wet examples. Repeat placements, save the raw range and define valid/ambiguous readings. Do not invent a universal percentage or label voltage as volumetric soil moisture.</p><p><strong>Contact testing</strong> means proving the probe really touches the intended patch consistently without hitting the plastic or crushing the crop. Include “hovering/not touching” trials: they must be INVALID, not interpreted as dry. A compliant printed holder/depth stop helps; software cannot correct a probe that misses the paper.</p><p>For light, extend the paddle to canopy height, face upward, avoid shadows, stop moving and collect fresh conversions. Start with roughly ten readings over two seconds at default timing, logging median/spread and pose. Reject stale/saturated/bus-error samples. Repeated readings improve repeatability, not absolute calibration; lux is not PPFD. <a href="https://www.mouser.com/datasheet/2/348/bh1750fvi-e-186247.pdf">ROHM datasheet</a>.</p>
<h2>Power and cameras still need commissioning</h2><p>Start from the robot’s supplied wall adapters; check that the actual labels accept Japan’s 100V mains. The owned 288Wh C300 DC is not an already completed motor-power solution: its connector, voltage/current capacity and protection path must match the robot. Do not assume USB-C alone supplies fixed 12V motor power.</p><p>A fixed overview camera would need its own mount and cable path back to a computer; a mobile robot cannot remain tethered to a fixed camera indefinitely. Our initial choice avoids that extra camera: use the existing head/wrist views and validate that they cover the watering area. No Raspberry Pi, depth camera, scale or pump purchase is required for the initial setup shown here.</p>
''')
