page('japan','Bring the experiment, not a live crop.','Carry-on only, ANA. Use the New York week for evidence; start a fresh local crop after arrival.', '''
<figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/dry-packing.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/dry-packing.png" alt="Blender illustration of cleaned dry prints and electronics in an open case"></a><figcaption>Clean, dry prints and an empty bottle only. The render is not a packing clearance or size guarantee; follow the item-specific travel notes below. Open the image for a larger view.</figcaption></figure><h2>Pack / source / verify</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Plan</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Printed kits and dry electronics</td><td>Clean, dry and protect; keep connections identified.</td><td>Remove growing material and water. Protect sensor ends and loose battery terminals.</td></tr><tr><td>Seed and used paper / soil / plants</td><td>NY test stays in the US; buy Japan seed locally.</td><td>Japan plant quarantine regulates seeds/plants and prohibits soil. We have not obtained import documents.</td></tr><tr><td>Bottles</td><td>Pack empty.</td><td>Refill after arrival; do not carry irrigation water.</td></tr><tr><td>Pointed soldering iron and hand tools</td><td>Borrow / buy in Japan.</td><td>ANA explicitly excludes pointed soldering irons and broadly restricts tools in carry-on.</td></tr><tr><td>Multimeter and pointed probes</td><td>Borrow / buy in Japan unless ANA confirms the exact kit.</td><td>No explicit ANA classification was found. This is uncertainty, not proof every meter is banned.</td></tr><tr><td>Anker C300 DC, 288Wh</td><td>Destination power equipment; do not pack for this passenger flight.</td><td>Exceeds the normal passenger lithium-battery limits. Being owned does not establish motor wiring compatibility.</td></tr><tr><td>Growing mats</td><td>Optional. Use household paper for NY; source locally if needed later.</td><td>Current cart delivery Sep 30 misses the NY trial. Exact processed wood-fiber customs treatment was not individually cleared.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><a href="https://www.ana.co.jp/en/jp/guide/boarding-procedures/baggage/international/caution-restriction02/">ANA tool restrictions</a> · <a href="https://www.ana.co.jp/en/jp/guide/boarding-procedures/baggage/international/caution-restriction03/">ANA conditional items / batteries / soldering irons</a> · <a href="https://www.faa.gov/hazmat/packsafe/batteries">FAA ordinary dry batteries (including 9V)</a> · <a href="https://www.maff.go.jp/pps/j/introduction/english.html">MAFF plant quarantine</a>. Recheck the actual itinerary; codeshare and departure-country rules also apply.</p>
<h2>Before leaving New York</h2><ol><li>Unjam the K1 Max and print plate A if time allows; otherwise carry the model files and print at the destination or use a household dish.</li><li>Clean and dry any prints; record distortion and snap-fit damage.</li><li>Pack model files, the Phase 0 software, ESP32 firmware and notes. Keep data backed up separately.</li><li>Arrange the Japan work surface/cart, seeds, basic tools and suitable electrical supplies. Finish modular-table geometry only after the correct files and reach measurements are available.</li></ol>
<h2>First Japan session</h2><p>Confirm delivered kit contents, power-adapter labels and cable types. Follow the numbered robot assembly guide, then add the dry light-sensor bench. Test cameras, serial devices, supply voltages and wiring continuity before wet operation. Then commission dummy trays and very small supervised pours. The first crop is sown here; no growing test preceded it.</p>
''')
page('research','Reuse the parts that have evidence.','Original models, upstream demonstrations, the experiment design and downloadable project files—without confusing proposals with proven behavior.', '''
<div class="toc"><a href="#upstream">Existing work</a><a href="#experiment">Crop experiment</a><a href="#downloads">Files</a><a href="#decisions">Decision history</a></div>
<section id="upstream"><h2>What pre-existing work gives us</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Useful contribution</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="https://xlerobot.readthedocs.io/en/latest/demos/index.html">XLeRobot showcase</a></td><td>Plant watering at ~56–59 seconds uses a tipped bottle. Clip labels it “Teleoped with Switch Joycons”.</td><td>Does not establish a ready autonomous plant-watering policy; spray-trigger use was not demonstrated in that segment.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://xlerobot.readthedocs.io/en/latest/software/getting_started/VLA_ACT.html">Official ACT guide</a></td><td>Record task demonstrations, train and evaluate; consistent camera views matter.</td><td>We need our own bottle/target data and evaluation. A Mac sensor logger is not proof every training workload runs locally.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://xlerobot.readthedocs.io/en/latest/software/getting_started/XLeRobot_teleop.html">Official teleoperation</a></td><td>Starting point for supervised arm motion.</td><td>Control scripts are not crop-care policies.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/434505-self-watering-cress-or-microgreens-planter">Macce cress planter</a></td><td>Printable wick-holder design using household paper.</td><td>Our PLA print still needs fit/leak testing.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/1287698-microgreens-germination-tray-kit">HomestteadAce kit</a></td><td>Outer tray, perforated insert and germination cover.</td><td>Medium contact and watering access depend on our setup.</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/177153-modular-table-100-printed/files">Aj_Awesome1 modular table</a></td><td>Free CC0 snap-together furniture parts.</td><td>Editable files not yet obtained; no verified load rating or configured K1 Max print estimate.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Prior wider research considered FarmBot, OpenFarm, GreenPiThumb, GardenBot, MudPi and agricultural rovers. They offer useful control/logging or garden-system ideas; none is evidence of a drop-in policy for this particular arm, bottle and paper tray. <a href="downloads/existing-work-historical.md">Read the dated broader research</a>.</p><p>Two water-named datasets inspected in the earlier review describe drink/bottle transport, not irrigation. No ready-to-run plant-watering checkpoint or multi-day care evaluation was located in that bounded review. <a href="downloads/watering-research.md">Source-by-source watering findings</a> · <a href="https://vector-wangel.github.io/XLeRobot-assets/videos/Real_demos/xlerobot030.mp4#t=56,60">Watch the watering clip</a>.</p></section>
<section id="experiment"><h2>Why one crop first?</h2><p>Use the same garden-cress lot for both NY kit tests. This makes water-path and handling problems easier to interpret than a mix of plants with different germination rates. Compare like-sized seeded patches and similar light; different tray geometries mean this remains a practical pilot, not an isolated causal test.</p><p>In Japan, use repeated batches of the same fast crop for robot-assisted versus human-baseline care. Assign comparable positions, keep seed lot/medium/density/recipe consistent, log all interventions, and distinguish tray-level outcomes from individual-seed counts. The value of multiple waves is checking whether a result repeats—not collecting variety for its own sake. Add another species later only to test a specific generalization question.</p><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Crop outcomes</h3><p>Emergence timing, visible survival/coverage, photos at fixed positions, healthy seed leaves, moisture consistency and the actual harvest when available.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Robot outcomes</h3><p>Valid sensing, correct target, successful/unknown delivery, spills, recoveries, human interventions and review time. A green crop alone does not prove autonomous care.</p></article></div><p>Use a manual care baseline. Do not withhold necessary water to preserve a comparison; intervene and record it. Six NY days can establish feasibility and expose failures, not prove a statistically reliable autonomy advantage.</p></section>
<section id="downloads"><h2>Downloads and source trail</h2><ul><li><a href="downloads/farm-visual-atlas.blend">Blender visual atlas: 17 editable scenes</a> — wiring placement, tools, planter cutaway, care storyboard and more.</li><li><a href="downloads/farm-modular-table-v3.blend">Blender assembly</a> — actual planter meshes, proxy table/robot.</li><li><a href="downloads/planter-source-stls.zip">Planter STL pack + license credits</a> — two creators, nine printed instances from six unique source files.</li><li><a href="downloads/farm-two-kits-three-plates.3mf">Editable Creality three-plate project</a> — inspect profile/settings before slicing.</li><li><a href="downloads/tool-prototypes-v2.zip">Tool-holder prototype STLs</a> — measured fit and probe compliance still pending.</li><li><a href="downloads/software-roadmap-detail.html">Detailed software roadmap</a> — updated current furniture note; engineering proposal.</li><li><a href="data/shopping.json">Shopping snapshot and per-item reasons</a> — last verified Sep 22; no personal items.</li><li><a href="xlerobot-deck.html">Original roadmap deck, archived</a> — older decisions, prominent superseded notice.</li><li><a href="sensor-shopping.html">Original sensor checklist, archived</a> — historical only.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/RonTuretzky/xlerobot-farm">Website source repository</a>.</li></ul><p>Planter models: Macce / HomestteadAce, CC BY-SA 4.0; see <a href="downloads/MODEL-CREDITS.md">model credits</a>. Assembly rendering/arrangement includes those models and is distributed under CC BY-SA 4.0. Original downloadable table selection is CC0. The site includes no raw private conversation transcripts or account addresses.</p></section>
<section id="decisions"><h2>What changed from older plans</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Older idea</th><th>Current decision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Likely Bambu A1</td><td>Available printer is Creality K1 Max 994A. Do not use another machine’s pre-sliced files.</td></tr><tr><td>Scale / fixed pump station</td><td>In-place sensing and robot-held bottle. No scale or electric pump in V0.</td></tr><tr><td>External water reservoir</td><td>Small manually refilled bottle and planter’s internal water pocket.</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed overhead camera</td><td>Robot head/wrist cameras first, with verified view coverage.</td></tr><tr><td>Many sensors attached at once</td><td>One task tool at a time; optional air sensor off the arm.</td></tr><tr><td>Low eight-pod stand on furniture</td><td>Two actual downloaded kits; free full modular table selected, exact parts still pending.</td></tr><tr><td>NY print and growing tests before travel</td><td>Attempted; the printer jammed and nothing was printed or sown. Software work on the Mac now; robot commissioning September 30.</td></tr><tr><td>Autonomous demo already solves watering</td><td>Verified clip is teleoperated; collect/evaluate our own task data.</td></tr><tr><td>Leak pads / extra measuring and grip supplies</td><td>Leak pads deferred, requested mat/rubber/labels/syringes removed. Use household measurement/containment.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The original PowerPoint redline emphasized dependable care cycles, evidence, remote recovery and model evaluation. Its scale/pump-specific suggestions have been translated into the current bottle profile rather than silently treated as shopping requirements.</p></section>
''')

